scenarios.final_text:
The performance problem in the loop is **avoidable reallocations from repeated appends**. Because `std::string out` starts empty and grows incrementally as each part is appended, the underlying buffer may be reallocated and copied multiple times.

The idiomatic fix is to **reserve** the total required memory upfront since the total size can be calculated before the loop begins.

```cpp
std::string join(const std::vector<std::string>& parts) {
  size_t total_size = 0;
  for (const auto& p : parts) total_size += p.size();

  std::string out;
  out.reserve(total_size); // Idiomatic fix
  for (const auto& p : parts) out += p;
  return out;
}
```

